= Place: Ming City Wall Ruins Park

_Use for: Describe a historical place near where you live._

I'd like to talk about a historical place near my home—the Ming City Wall Ruins Park. It's about a 20-minute walk from where I live in Beijing.

This park has a surviving section of the old city wall, built in the 1400s during the Ming Dynasty. About 1.5 kilometers remain. The wall is made of large grey bricks worn down by centuries of weather. On top, you can still see the old battlements and the remains of barracks where soldiers once stayed. Walking along it, you can imagine how strong it must have been. It actually reminds me of the Black Wall in _Game of Thrones_.

There's also an old cannon on top—big, black, and solid, with a barrel about 30 centimeters wide. My six-year-old son loves to climb on it. I've taken so many photos of him there.

What I love most is the contrast. From that ancient wall, you can see Beijing Railway Station in the distance, with modern bullet trains coming and going. You're touching something over 600 years old, but right next to you is modern China. It gives me a strong sense of history passing by.

I'd recommend this place to any visitor. It's not crowded, it's free, and it offers a beautiful view of old and new Beijing together.

= Experience: Roller Coaster at Universal Beijing

_Use for: Describe a thrilling experience / a time you felt scared._

I'd like to talk about an exciting experience I had about five years ago at Universal Beijing Resort. It was a company outing, and it was my first roller coaster ride ever—the Decepticoaster in the Transformers area.

I was nervous but excited while waiting in line. Some colleagues opted out, which made me more determined to try it. Excitement won over fear.

What makes this coaster so scary is the start. Unlike normal ones that go up slowly, this one launches you from zero to top speed in seconds. There's no time to prepare—you're just thrown into it.

The rest of the ride was intense too—loops, drops, and sharp turns. My body was swung side to side. Tears came from my eyes because of the wind, but I refused to close them—I didn't want to miss anything. My heart pounded and I gripped the handles tightly, but I loved every second.

When it stopped, I was dizzy but thrilled. I enjoyed it so much that I went straight back for a second go. It was unforgettable—terrifying and exciting at the same time. If you visit Universal Beijing, I highly recommend it, especially if you're brave enough for that launch.

= Person: My Father

_Use for: Describe a person who surprised you / a family member you admire._

I'd like to talk about someone I used to underestimate—my father. Born in 1951, he worked as a mid-level manager in a state-owned factory. He's a serious man, always focused on rules. I used to make fun of him, thinking he was all talk and no action. But in 2021, he changed my view completely.

That year, our apartment building was scheduled for demolition. The developer offered money to all residents, and most neighbors took it and moved out quickly. But my father noticed something they missed: under the law, the developer also had to pay our rent during the period between moving out and moving into a new home. He refused to move, delayed the project, and the case went to court.

I was worried. Here was a man with no legal training, up against a big company with lawyers. I feared we would lose everything. But throughout the hearings, my father stayed calm. He didn't hire a lawyer. Instead, he collected every government document he could find until he had built a very strong case.

In the end, the developer realized they couldn't win. They offered a settlement with very good terms—money and a new home. My father accepted. I felt relieved, and more than that, proud.

This taught me that my father isn't just serious—he's patient, careful, and brave when it comes to his family. He doesn't show off; he just does the work and stands up for what's right. That's what a real hero looks like.

= Object: My Drone

_Use for: Describe an object that is important to you / a piece of technology you used._

I'd like to talk about a small drone I won as a prize at my company's annual party a few years ago. It was palm-sized, with a white body and four black rotor blades. It looked surprisingly nice for a toy.

The drone had a camera and an app that turned my phone into a controller, with a live view from above. Using it felt like playing a video game. I used it a lot at first—filming family events and taking aerial photos of my neighborhood. The best moment was watching my young son laugh with joy when he first saw it take off with that loud buzz.

But I wasn't a good pilot, so it crashed often—even without trying the 360-degree flips. I broke a few plastic blades, but spare ones came in the package. Over time, I lost interest and gave it to my cousin.

Looking back, what made it special wasn't the technology, but the happiness it brought, especially to my son. And even if I hadn't given it away, I couldn't fly it now—Beijing has recently banned civilian drones. So it's just a nice memory of a simple thrill.
